VERDICT: OVERRULE
CHECKER: judge-standards
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: P16
ATTEMPT: 1
---
Ruling on the letter of LEDGER_P16_P18_SPEC.md § "P16", not on whether the
underlying gap in migration.go is worth fixing (it may be; see below).

1. Criterion 3's actual text, not just its header, decides this. The header
   reads "Both sides of every mutation invalidate" — that phrase alone would
   support checker-second's reading. But the body immediately narrows it:
   "Each write path — the atomic replace, `CreateExclusive`, and `Remove` —
   invalidates before the disk change and again after it. Name the line for
   each; a path that invalidates only once fails this criterion." This is an
   enumeration with a named failure condition ("invalidates only once"),
   applied to three named paths — not "any write path in the package that
   never invalidates fails this criterion." checker-tests named the six
   required line numbers for exactly those three paths (storage.go:387, 401,
   452, 672/674) and checker-second's own item 5 independently confirms the
   same three are correctly ordered. Criterion 3, read as written, is met.

2. The "Note for the second lane" is the only textual hook broad enough to
   reach migration.go, and it does not reach that far. It reads: "this file
   was merged by hand on 2026-08-22 (2a5c068) against a conflicting refactor
   that split encryption into encodeForWrite. The merge resolution is in
   scope. In particular, ask whether any write path reaches disk through a
   code path that skips the generation bump." "This file" is storage.go — the
   merge commit's own message says so explicitly ("internal/services/
   storage/storage.go was the only semantic one" of the conflicts) and
   describes only encodeForWrite, writeFileLocked and CreateExclusive.
   migration.go was not touched by 2a5c068, was not part of the conflict,
   and predates 06176ef by a separate feature history (age encryption,
   c07cb4c onward). The note widened where to look inside the merged file's
   write paths — it did not license auditing every write path in the package
   regardless of the merge. checker-tests answered the note's actual
   question (grep for encodeForWrite call sites, confirming the split did
   not create a skipped-invalidation path); checker-second answered a
   different, broader question the note does not ask.

3. The manifest (storage.go, cache_test.go) does not by itself bar a
   checker from citing an adjacent defect — nothing in CLAUDE.md says a
   FAIL is invalid merely for naming a file outside the manifest, and
   checker-tests.md's own scope instruction ("nothing modified outside what
   the brief authorizes") governs what the *worker* may touch, not what a
   checker may look at. So the manifest boundary alone would not decide this
   dispute either way; criterion 3's own enumerated scope is what decides it
   (point 1).

4. This is the same shape as the S4 precedent, not a different one.
   ACCESSIBILITY.md point 16 records that a FAIL resting on an "implicit
   companion requirement" no numbered point carried was overruled, and the
   defect class was made citable only by adding new text afterward.
   Criterion 3 here names its own scope in exactly the way that dispute
   found missing; checker-second's FAIL extends "every mutation" past what
   the criterion's own enumeration and the second-lane note support. Per
   that precedent, the fix is to overrule and add text, not to uphold a FAIL
   the current text does not carry.

5. On the merits, for completeness: checker-second's own evidence weakens
   the claim further. Its first reproduction (real EnableEncryption/
   DisableEncryption calls) shows the cache entry lingers and cacheGen is
   unmoved, but does not show a stale ReadFile — because, as checker-tests
   found and checker-second does not dispute, encryption/decryption changes
   file size, so the mtime+size cache key misses and the next read goes to
   disk correctly. To get an actual stale-read, checker-second substituted
   a bare os.WriteFile equal-length rewrite standing in for migration.go's
   writer, explicitly noting "migration.go's real writer is unreachable
   from the test package boundary." That is evidence about a hypothetical
   same-size migration writer, not about migration.go as it exists. The gap
   in the package is real (no invalidateCache call sites in migration.go);
   the specific stale-read hazard criterion 1 is about is not demonstrated
   through it.

FAIL is set aside. checker-tests' PASS stands as the operative verdict for
P16 at this attempt.

Recommendation (the amendment this defect needs to become citable, matching
how point 16 was added after S4): add a criterion, or a project-wide point in
a storage/caching standard, requiring that every function in the storage
package that rewrites a file at a cached path call invalidateCache on both
sides, not only the three paths reachable through Storage's own write API.
migration.go's encrypt/decrypt/rollback helpers should be named explicitly.
Until that text exists, a checker can flag the migration.go gap as a defect
report to the lead (as checker-tests did) but cannot fail P16's criterion 3
on it.
